    {
      "number": 14,
      "id": "I_kwDORpjjVc8AAAABB3dNTg",
      "title": "draft-birkholz-cose-receipts-ccf-profile-05: Missing hash for initial value of h",
      "url": "https://github.com/ietf-wg-scitt/draft-ietf-scitt-receipts-ccf-profile/issues/14",
      "state": "CLOSED",
      "author": "micromaomao",
      "authorAssociation": "NONE",
      "assignees": [
        "achamayou",
        "Copilot"
      ],
      "labels": [],
      "body": "In https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-birkholz-cose-receipts-ccf-profile-05.html, when it says\n\n```\n     h := proof.leaf.internal-transaction-hash\n          || HASH(proof.leaf.internal-evidence)\n          || proof.leaf.data-hash\n```\n\nit should say\n\n```\n     h := HASH(\n          proof.leaf.internal-transaction-hash\n              || HASH(proof.leaf.internal-evidence)\n              || proof.leaf.data-hash\n     )\n```\n\n(ref: https://github.com/microsoft/scitt-ccf-ledger/blob/main/pyscitt/pyscitt/receipt.py#L132-L134)",
      "createdAt": "2026-05-11T10:57:53Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-05-13T15:35:15Z",
      "closedAt": "2026-05-13T15:35:15Z",
      "comments": []
    },
    {
      "number": 18,
      "id": "I_kwDORpjjVc8AAAABDdS9rg",
      "title": "Hash algorithm for transaction components",
      "url": "https://github.com/ietf-wg-scitt/draft-ietf-scitt-receipts-ccf-profile/issues/18",
      "state": "OPEN",
      "author": "micromaomao",
      "authorAssociation": "NONE",
      "assignees": [],
      "labels": [],
      "body": "Section 2.1 says that\n\n> A CCF ledger is a binary Merkle Tree constructed from a hash function H, which is defined from the log type. For instance, the hash function for CCF_LEDGER_SHA256 is SHA256, whose HASH_SIZE is 32 bytes.\n\nWhich clarifies that the hash function _used for the merkle tree_ is sha256. However, section 2.2 does not have an equivalent clarification for the hash function used for internal-transaction-hash and (more crucially) data-hash (i.e. it's implied that when it says \"data-hash summarizes the application data...\" this summarization is done using the same function as the Merkle Tree, or done with sha256 as defined by CCF_LEDGER_SHA256)\n",
      "createdAt": "2026-05-26T19:45:06Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-05-26T19:45:06Z",
      "closedAt": null,
      "comments": []
    }
  ],
